"""Report the isolated single-context p8 pipeline experiment."""
from gpt2_report_sections import table
from figure_support import add_figure


def add_pipeline_results(doc, heading, body, caption):
    p = heading(doc, 'XV. Dependent p8 round pipelining and controller timing')
    p.paragraph_format.page_break_before = True
    body(doc, 'The isolated one-round experiment found no latency benefit from splitting a dependent round. The best tested design retains the original one-round datapath and optimizes its controller: 12 clocks at a 4.15 ns passing routed period, or 49.8 ns per block, compared with 70.8 ns for baseline A. This is a 1.422 times elapsed-time improvement. These are routed periods multiplied by simulated cycle counts; no pipeline variant was programmed or measured on the FPGA [19].')
    body(doc, 'This experiment starts from main commit 3cceb3c and uses one message context, unchanged nonce semantics and no round unrolling or interleaving. It is separate from the folded and multicore designs. A denotes accept and absorb, O the output handshake, L the permutation load, and C the final capture. The schedule A, O, L, eight p8 rounds with constants B4 through 4B, C gives the measured 12-clock block interval. A two-stage reused round needs 20 clocks and a three-stage reused round needs 28. Initialization and finalization still use p12, with 12, 24 or 36 round clocks respectively.')
    add_figure(doc, 'fig_xv_pipeline_rounds.png', 'Fig. 9. Implemented round boundaries for A, B and C. Each stage result is registered; the P register holds only completed rounds. Dependent rounds do not overlap. D uses B with controller fixes; A_ctrl2 retains A.', caption)
    table(doc, ['Variant', 'Cycles', 'Pass / next fail ns', 'MHz', 'Block ns', 'Speedup'], [
        ('A baseline', 12, '5.90 / 5.85', '169.5', '70.8', '1.000'),
        ('B two-stage', 20, '6.20 / 6.15', '161.3', '124.0', '0.571'),
        ('C three-stage', 28, '6.35 / 6.30', '157.5', '177.8', '0.398'),
        ('D B plus fixes', 20, '4.30 / 4.25', '232.6', '86.0', '0.823'),
        ('A_ctrl1 decode', 12, '4.70 / 4.65', '212.8', '56.4', '1.255'),
        ('A_ctrl2 all fixes', 12, '4.15 / 4.10', '241.0', '49.8', '1.422'),
    ], [1.77, .55, 1.68, .8, .97, 1.37])
    caption(doc, 'Table XIII. Best tested passing periods at 0.05 ns resolution. Speedup is 70.8 ns divided by block latency. A, D and A_ctrl2 use explore; the others use default [19].')
    heading(doc, 'A. Critical paths and the control experiment')
    body(doc, 'At baseline A\'s 5.90 ns target, the round has about 1.9 ns of spare time. The limiting path is in_keep decoding into wide state-register enables: six logic levels, approximately 1.2 ns logic and 4.7 ns reported routing, with fanout 129. Round splitting preserves this limit while adding registers and control load. B and C reach only about 0.97 and 0.94 times A\'s frequency, against required ratios above 20/12 = 1.667 and 28/12 = 2.333. C\'s stages already take about 2.4 to 2.5 ns each; balancing those stages alone does not remove the limiting control path. Reported routing fractions are Vivado categories and do not imply that all net delay is removable wire delay [17].')
    body(doc, 'D was built on B because its measured latency was lower than C\'s. Control optimization raises D to 232.6 MHz, or 1.37 times baseline A\'s frequency, but its 20 clocks still take 86.0 ns. Applying the same changes to A is the decisive control experiment. Per-lane keep decoding removes keep-count and validation logic from wide enables; default-strategy frequency rises from 166.7 to 212.8 MHz. A one-hot controller and a tree-shaped tag compare, five levels instead of thirteen, complete A_ctrl2. The combined changes reach 241.0 MHz at the same 12 clocks. B adds 315 core registers and C adds 638; A_ctrl2 uses 2,271 LUTs and 1,090 registers versus A\'s 2,369 and 1,080.')
    add_figure(doc, 'fig_xv_pipeline_control.png', 'Fig. 10. Controller changes that produce the measured gain. Gray blocks show local keep decoding, one-hot control and the tree tag compare. A_ctrl2 keeps the single-clock round and both original state owners; command, padding and output details are omitted.', caption)
    body(doc, 'Explore improves the best periods by only a few percent: A 6.00 to 5.90 ns, A_ctrl2 4.30 to 4.15 ns, and D 4.50 to 4.30 ns. Retiming never improves these results and more than doubles A\'s register count. A_ctrl2\'s remaining limits include tag comparison, controller and done fanout, and the round itself, now only 0.48 ns from critical. A 20-cycle candidate must close every path below 4.15 times 12/20 = 2.49 ns, above 401.6 MHz, to beat A_ctrl2. The 241 MHz result is the highest tested pass, not a measured hardware maximum.')
    heading(doc, 'B. Verification and measurement boundaries')
    body(doc, 'Each final variant passes 2,178 official directional checks, 400 per-round permutation runs, 520 unstalled streaming transactions and 520 transactions with random stalls. Tests cover empty, partial and full blocks, 1 KiB and 4 KiB messages, forged tags, and resets throughout the pipeline phases and during transactions. Saved logs also cover D_alt_3stage, which was verified but not timing-swept. Encryption and decryption have identical unstalled cycle counts. With no AD, A_ctrl2 takes 801 clocks or 3,324.2 ns for 1 KiB, and 3,105 clocks or 12,885.8 ns for 4 KiB; these are message simulations, not complete-adapter or memory-transfer measurements.')
    body(doc, 'All twelve reported best implementation runs have nonnegative setup, hold and pulse-width slack, zero routing errors, zero DRC errors and zero DRC critical warnings. No timing exceptions are used. Internal endpoints are constrained; the asynchronous button input and LED output are the only unconstrained I/O. The registered timing harness prevents unused logic from disappearing. Source hashes and comment-stripped pre-relabel hashes preserve measurement provenance. The saved next failing period brackets each reported passing point but does not prove a global frequency limit. Faster board operation requires an MMCM or PLL and reset held until synchronized lock; editing create_clock does not change the oscillator.')
